request: Rename the `watched` field of watchlist items to `seen` everywhere.

governor turn 1: plan -> movie, watchlist-store, watchlist, search, movie-detail, app  (Rename the watchlist-item status field `WatchItem.watched` → `WatchItem.seen` and propagate it everywhere: the type change lives in movie, watchlist-store re-implements add/toggle/count plus a migration of already-persisted items (so no saved status is lost), the watchlist UI updates its bindings, and the remaining store consumers audit for references. Method/signal names (`toggleWatched`, `watchedCount`) and user-visible copy stay unchanged — the request targets the data field, not identifiers. The legacy `Movie` interface keeps its own `watched` field since it is not a watchlist item; say the word if you want that one renamed too.)
round 1: owners movie=ok watchlist-store=fail watchlist=rejected search=ok movie-detail=ok app=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist-store, watchlist, movie  (Round 1 partially applied: movie's WatchItem rename is in, but three errors remain. watchlist-store's owner failed before applying anything (its file still uses `watched`); watchlist's change was correct but rejected pending approval of one test assertion; and movie's new spec test has a compile error. I approve the watchlist test change (behaviour unchanged, only the field name) and re-instruct those three units, keeping instructions concise to avoid another max_tokens failure.)
round 2: owners watchlist-store=ok watchlist=ok movie=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist-store  (tsc is clean and every unit's own spec passes; the only failures are the governor's watchlist-store acceptance tests, which threw NG0203 by constructing `new WatchlistStore()` outside an injection context while the store's constructor calls effect(). The unit code is correct — replace the acceptance test with a TestBed-based instantiation; no unit code changes needed.)
round 3: owners watchlist-store=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (Rename complete and verified: WatchItem now exposes `seen` (pinned at type level by the movie acceptance test), the store persists/reads `seen` and migrates legacy persisted `watched` data (pinned by the watchlist-store acceptance test), and tsc is clean with all 43 tests passing, so no code or template still references a `watched` item field. Method/signal names (`toggleWatched`, `watchedCount`) are not the field and were intentionally preserved; the unused `Movie` interface is not a watchlist item type (items are `WatchItem`), so its `watched` field is out of scope.)

